    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.Background">Background and system tray</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.StartSilently">Start silently (keep the main window hidden)</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.StartSilentlyNote">Applies on next launch, including sign-in startup. Capture launches still show the interface.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.MinimizeToTray">Hide to tray when minimized</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.CloseToTray">Close button hides to tray (turn off to exit)</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.TrayNote">Shortcuts keep working while hidden. Click the tray icon or choose Show window to restore; choose Exit to quit. Without a tray, silent startup is ignored and closing exits.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Tray.Show">Show window</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Tray.Exit">Exit</x:String>

    <!-- Excluded apps / elevation -->
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Hotkey.Action.PickExcludedApp">Pick an excluded app (click a window to add it)</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.Excluded">Hotkey exclusions</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedNote">Global hotkeys are suppressed while one of these programs has focus (useful for games, Task Manager, remote desktops). Entries can be a process name (taskmgr.exe) or a full exe path.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedForceLabel">Force-trigger modifiers</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedForcePlaceholder">e.g. Ctrl+Alt; empty = no hotkeys in excluded apps</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedForceNote">Inside an excluded app, hold these modifiers together with the hotkey to trigger it anyway (Ctrl+Alt by default). Hotkeys that already contain them (e.g. Ctrl+Alt+F5) are not restricted.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedAppPlaceholder">Process name (taskmgr.exe) or exe path</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedBrowse">Browse…</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedAdd">+ Add</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedEmpty">No excluded application yet.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedInvalid">Enter a process name or choose an exe file first.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedAdded">Added to the exclusion list: {0}</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedAlready">Already excluded: {0}</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedRemoved">Removed from the exclusion list.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedPickHint">Move over the target window and click to confirm (Esc / right-click cancels)</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedPickTarget">Target: {0} — click to exclude</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedPickCancelled">Selection cancelled.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedPickFailed">Could not pick the app: {0}</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedPickFile">Choose the program to exclude</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedPickFileType">Executable files</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ExcludedSuppressed">Last hotkey suppressed by the exclusion list: {0}</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.WindowDetectionToggle">Detect the window under the cursor while capturing (click to capture it)</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.WindowDetectionNote">Hovering highlights the window under the cursor (yellow box); a click captures that window, and dragging still selects a free region. Child elements inside it (buttons / inputs / list items) are not detected by default — turn on the next item if you need them.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElementDetectionToggle">Detect the child element under the cursor (orange box, click to capture it)</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElementDetectionNote">Off by default, so a click always captures the whole window. When on, an orange box appears for the child element and a click captures it; use the level key below to walk outwards, or hold the ignore key to capture the window. Custom-drawn surfaces (games, some browser content) usually only expose the window, so turning this on may change nothing there.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.WholeWindowOffScreenToggle">Capture the whole window when it extends off-screen</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.WholeWindowOffScreenNote">A screen grab only gets the part that is on screen; with this on, PrintWindow lets the window render itself so off-screen parts are captured too. A few GPU-drawn windows refuse to render — those fall back to the screen grab.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.WholeWindowOccludedToggle">Capture the whole window when it is covered by other windows</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.WholeWindowOccludedNote">A click-capture of a window also gets the parts hidden behind other foreground windows (again via PrintWindow). With this off you get exactly what is visible on screen, occluders included.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElementPadding">Element capture padding (pixels)</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElementPaddingNote">When a click captures a child element, its bounds grow (positive) or shrink (negative) by this many pixels; default +2. Only element captures are affected - whole-window captures ignore it. Hold the ignore key (Ctrl by default) and click to ignore child elements and capture the window instead.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElementLevelKey">Child element level key (walk outwards)</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElementLevelKeyNote">Switches which child element level a click will capture; every key here is configurable: wheel = scroll up to go one level outwards (parent), scroll down to go back inwards; tab = Tab outwards, Shift+Tab inwards; arrows = Up outwards, Down inwards; none = off (always the innermost element). At the window level the orange element box disappears and a click captures the whole window.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElementIgnoreModifier">Key to ignore child elements</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElementIgnoreNote">Holding it hides the element box and makes a click capture the whole window (Ctrl by default) - ignoring sub-elements and capturing the whole window are the same thing, so this is the only such key.</x:String>

    <!-- Elevation / elevated auto-start -->
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.Elevation">Privileges and admin windows</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElevationNote">Windows UIPI: hotkeys registered by a normal-privilege process are not delivered while an elevated window (Task Manager, admin cmd / PowerShell) has focus. Restarting as administrator fixes that; the trade-off is that drag-and-drop from Explorer stops working.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElevationStatusElevated">Running as administrator.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElevationStatusNormal">Running with normal privileges — hotkeys do not work while an admin window is in front.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElevationRestart">Restart as administrator</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElevationRestarting">Restarting as administrator…</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElevationFailed">Elevation failed: {0}</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElevatedAutoStart">Start on login with highest privileges (Task Scheduler)</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElevatedAutoStartNote">Starts silently as administrator at sign-in, without a UAC prompt. Toggling it asks for elevation once (UAC) - just click Yes. It replaces the normal auto-start.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElevatedAutoStartOn">Elevated auto-start enabled.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElevatedAutoStartOff">Elevated auto-start disabled.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Str.Settings.ElevatedAutoStartFailed">Failed: {0}</x:String>
</ResourceDictionary>
